  "cards": [
    {
      "title": "Different evidence",
      "items": [
        "Portable readers retain per-architecture signing metadata and hashes but do not verify signature integrity, trust, Gatekeeper acceptance or notarization.",
        "Native inspection verifies observed code-signing properties with macOS tools. Inspection never chooses allow or deny policy."
      ],
      "dot": "authentication"
    },
    {
      "title": "Artifact boundaries",
      "items": [
        "Artifact inspection is bounded and does not run installer scripts or mount disk images. Applications created or downloaded by scripts cannot be discovered from payload bytes.",
        "Container-relative locations are provenance, not installed paths. Partial inspection is reported explicitly; callers select an application and architecture."
      ],
      "dot": "artifact"
    },
    {
      "title": "From identity to device behavior",
      "items": [
        "Authoring consumes selected bundle IDs, signing identifiers, team identifiers, hashes or requirements as supported by the chosen payload.",
        "Target validation checks supported schema constraints. Publication and device activation remain separate steps; a successful authoring request does not establish physical enforcement."
      ],
      "dot": "policy"
    },
    {
      "dot": "transport",
      "title": "Public documentation",
      "items": [
        "Code-signing hashes \u2014 https://developer.apple.com/documentation/technotes/tn3126-inside-code-signing-hashes",
        "macOS code-signing requirements \u2014 https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/technotes/tn2206/_index.html"
      ]
    },
